    "en": {
      "shared_perspectives": "German media across the spectrum agree that the asylum and deportation apparatus is overloaded and poorly coordinated, producing processing bottlenecks that strain local authorities. They also share the view that irregular migration routes – notably the Mediterranean crossings and the Ceuta corridor – demand stronger EU‑wide border management and bilateral deals, even though the preferred tools differ. At the same time, reports from both left‑leaning and right‑leaning outlets acknowledge that Germany’s demographic and labour‑market challenges, especially in the eastern states, make a controlled flow of migrants indispensable, prompting calls for clearer legal frameworks.",
      "controversial_points": "The core conflict pits national security concerns against humanitarian obligations. Conservatives frame migration as a crime and cultural threat, demanding stricter borders and deportations. Liberals and left parties stress labor shortages and integration, arguing for open policies and EU solidarity. The debate is amplified by legal battles over asylum law and foreign‑policy ties, exposing sovereignty versus EU coordination."
